contributorReview.body:
@parth-opensrc, this overlap is possible in safe Go. Take two views of a `buf [4]*byte`: `buf[:3:3]` and `buf[1:4:4]`. As named slices, they round-trip in either traversal order. Convert the second to `*[3]*byte` and Save fails in both: slice-first reaches this check; pointer-first reaches the corresponding check in `sliceTarget`. Can the array pointer and slice share their restored backing storage just as the two slices do?

The [fixture on the integrated rewrite](https://github.com/tamird/gvisor/blob/bd3cab255359dcaa97607d78eac4ba4c59eb7299/pkg/state/tests/graph_test.go#L475) checks both shared slots, writes through one view, and the final pointee reachable only from the other. [Both failures](https://app.buildbuddy.io/invocation/0bffd576-7769-49b2-a0ce-e66cb7a4f0d6) are also recorded in the [fork run](https://github.com/tamird/gvisor/actions/runs/40795069761).

Posted by Codex on behalf of @tamird.
